WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.427
Most LLM inference can be understood as two related phases: prefill and decode.

00:00:04.427 --> 00:00:08.087
Prefill is the work of reading the prompt and existing context.

00:00:08.087 --> 00:00:11.416
If the user sends a long document, a repository excerpt,

00:00:11.416 --> 00:00:13.080
or a large instruction block,

00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:16.408
prefill can dominate the time before the model starts speaking.

00:00:16.408 --> 00:00:21.067
Decode is the phase that produces new tokens after the prompt has been processed.

00:00:21.067 --> 00:00:23.730
Batching often helps these phases in different ways.

00:00:23.730 --> 00:00:24.395
During prefill,

00:00:24.395 --> 00:00:28.389
the runtime may be able to process larger blocks of prompt tokens

00:00:28.389 --> 00:00:31.717
efficiently because there is a lot of parallel work available.

00:00:31.717 --> 00:00:32.383
During decode,

00:00:32.383 --> 00:00:36.709
each active sequence usually needs the next token before it can move forward,

00:00:36.709 --> 00:00:39.704
so the shape of the work is more incremental.

00:00:39.704 --> 00:00:44.031
That is why prompt processing speed and generation speed should be measured separately.

00:00:44.031 --> 00:00:49.023
First-token latency is the time between submitting the request and seeing the first generated token.

00:00:49.023 --> 00:00:52.351
It is strongly affected by prompt length, queueing, prefill efficiency,

00:00:52.351 --> 00:00:54.681
and how many other requests are active.

00:00:54.681 --> 00:00:58.675
Tokens per second measures generation speed once the model is producing output.

00:00:58.675 --> 00:01:03.334
Requests per second matters more for services that handle many users or automated clients.

00:01:03.334 --> 00:01:07.328
A workload made of long prompts and short answers behaves differently from

00:01:07.328 --> 00:01:10.323
a workload made of short prompts and long answers.

00:01:10.323 --> 00:01:12.653
Long prompts stress prefill and context memory.

00:01:12.653 --> 00:01:14.982
Long responses stress decode and sustained generation.

00:01:14.982 --> 00:01:17.645
Good tuning begins by naming the workload clearly,

00:01:17.645 --> 00:01:21.639
because a batch configuration is only good relative to the kind of

00:01:21.639 --> 00:01:23.968
requests the system actually needs to serve.

00:01:23.968 --> 00:01:25.299
When reviewing benchmark output,

00:01:25.299 --> 00:01:28.628
avoid collapsing prefill and decode into one vague speed number.

00:01:28.628 --> 00:01:33.287
A configuration can be excellent at absorbing a long prompt but ordinary during generation,

00:01:33.287 --> 00:01:34.951
or the other way around.

00:01:34.951 --> 00:01:38.945
Separate measurements help you decide whether to tune for faster first response,

00:01:38.945 --> 00:01:41.940
smoother streaming, or better total capacity under repeated requests.
